00:02:52.666 --> 00:02:54.500
Let's get a quick overview of Tokyo.

00:02:55.916 --> 00:02:58.291
The city's made of 23 special wards,

00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:01.958
kind of like districts with their own
centers, attractions and feel.

00:03:02.791 --> 00:03:04.041
You don't need to know all of them,

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.791
so let's focus on the main
areas worth visiting.

00:03:07.458 --> 00:03:09.083
This includes the wards of Shibuya,

00:03:09.666 --> 00:03:14.875
Shinjuku, Minato, Chiyoda, Chuo,
Taito and Sumida.

00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:17.791
Let's talk about where to stay in Tokyo.

00:03:18.166 --> 00:03:20.125
Here are a few areas I usually recommend.

00:03:21.583 --> 00:03:23.958
Shibuya is one of the most iconic
spots in the city.

00:03:24.541 --> 00:03:26.375
Home to the busiest crosswalk
in the world,

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.625
it's a busy high-energy area packed with
shops, restaurants and bars.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.416
If you're looking for a lively
atmosphere and

00:03:33.416 --> 00:03:36.291
plenty to do at night, Shibuya
is a solid choice.

00:03:36.958 --> 00:03:39.416
Shinjuku is another popular
area known for

00:03:39.416 --> 00:03:41.708
Kabukicho, Tokyo's entertainment district.

00:03:42.291 --> 00:03:46.125
Think near-lit back streets, hidden
bars and loud late-night fun.

00:03:46.708 --> 00:03:49.333
It's great if you want a memorable
nightlife experience,

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.708
but it might not be the best
fit for families.

00:03:52.166 --> 00:03:55.833
Asakusa is the old town district,
centered around Senso-ji Temple.

00:03:56.375 --> 00:03:58.833
It's quieter, more traditional
and a good option

00:03:58.833 --> 00:04:01.583
if you're into culture or want
a slower-paced stay.

00:04:01.916 --> 00:04:04.791
Just keep in mind it's a bit further
out from the city center,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.958
so do account for some added travel time.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.125
And finally, Ginza.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:12.041
It's Tokyo's upscaled shopping
and dining district.

00:04:12.583 --> 00:04:15.500
It's centrally located, which makes it
convenient for getting around,

00:04:15.875 --> 00:04:18.458
but the area itself is more
refined and low-key.

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:20.708
If you're after something
with more energy,

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:22.875
then the other neighborhoods
might be a better fit.

00:04:23.375 --> 00:04:27.583
As for hotel recommendation, I really like
staying at the Muji Hotel in Ginza.

00:04:28.083 --> 00:04:30.041
I stayed there on a previous
trip and found

00:04:30.041 --> 00:04:32.208
the rooms super comfortable
and nicely designed.

00:04:32.583 --> 00:04:35.708
You also get to use Muji-branded amenities,
which is a nice touch.

00:04:36.541 --> 00:04:38.916
So if you're a Muji fan or just
want a clean, cozy

00:04:38.916 --> 00:04:41.625
place to stay, it's definitely
a place to check out.

00:04:43.333 --> 00:04:46.875
Let's head to Shibuya and begin
at the Shibuya Crossing.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.125
You can't visit Tokyo without experiencing
the famous Shibuya Crossing,

00:05:02.583 --> 00:05:04.916
the world's busiest pedestrian
intersection.

00:05:06.041 --> 00:05:10.166
It's estimated that around 300,000 people
pass through it every single day.

00:05:10.833 --> 00:05:13.291
It's chaotic, but in the
best way possible.

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.875
For a great view of the crossing,
head to the

00:05:16.875 --> 00:05:19.666
Mag's Park rooftop for a unique
top-down perspective.

00:05:30.416 --> 00:05:33.041
Another spot with a stunning
view is Shibuya Sky,

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:37.958
a rooftop observatory with 360 degrees
view of Tokyo's skyline.

00:05:38.541 --> 00:05:42.791
On a clear day, you can even catch a glimpse
of Mount Fuji in the distance.

00:05:44.291 --> 00:05:47.333
The place is especially popular
around sunset hours,

00:05:47.666 --> 00:05:49.125
so if you want to visit during that time,

00:05:49.541 --> 00:05:52.125
booking a few weeks in advance
is a good idea.

00:06:01.916 --> 00:06:06.916
Next, check out the Miyashita Park, a modern
urban space built above a train line.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.416
It blends shopping, dining, and
a rooftop park all in one.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.000
Below deck you can browse
the many stores.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:24.333
One of my favorite ones is GBL, which
sells limited edition Ghibli merch.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.500
As a fan, I couldn't resist adding
to my small collection.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:39.333
If you're into gaming or anime, Shibuya
Parco is worth a visit.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.291
It's home to the official Nintendo
store and the

00:06:42.291 --> 00:06:45.333
Pokemon Center, both great spots
for exclusive merch.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.291
On the other side of Shibuya,
you'll find Yoyogi Park.

00:06:53.041 --> 00:06:56.333
It's the main park of Shibuya and a
peaceful retreat from the city.

00:06:57.166 --> 00:06:59.750
It's especially beautiful during
the cherry blossom season.

00:07:07.208 --> 00:07:10.000
Within the park, you can also
find the Meiji Shrine,

00:07:10.625 --> 00:07:15.250
a serene Shinto shrine tucked into a forested
area with peaceful walking paths.

00:07:29.708 --> 00:07:31.458
Right next door is Harajuku,

00:07:32.083 --> 00:07:35.166
Tokyo's hotspot for youth culture
and colorful fashion.

00:07:35.916 --> 00:07:37.958
The main draw here is the
Takeshita Street.

00:07:40.291 --> 00:07:43.916
The street is packed with street food
offerings, quirky fashion boutiques,

00:07:44.166 --> 00:07:47.666
and themed animal cafes like the Mipig Cafe,

00:07:47.958 --> 00:07:50.416
where you can hang out with adorable micro pigs.

00:07:57.333 --> 00:08:01.583
If animal cafes aren't your thing, 
try some of the fun street snacks instead.

00:08:09.833 --> 00:08:11.583
We then move into Omotesando,

00:08:11.875 --> 00:08:14.333
a stylish, tree-lined avenue known for

00:08:14.333 --> 00:08:16.666
its luxury boutiques and
sleek architecture.

00:08:18.333 --> 00:08:21.625
One spot I always return to
here is Higuma Doughnuts

00:08:22.416 --> 00:08:25.750
a small, cozy cafe that combines
two Tokyo favorites,

00:08:26.291 --> 00:08:28.583
handmade donuts and specialty coffee.

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:31.458
They use high-quality Hokkaido
ingredients,

00:08:31.833 --> 00:08:34.583
which gives their donuts a super
light and fluffy texture.

00:08:39.041 --> 00:08:42.541
And honestly, it might just be the
best donut shop in Tokyo,

00:08:42.958 --> 00:08:43.958
at least in my opinion.

00:08:48.375 --> 00:08:52.375
If you're craving sushi, then check out
Kaiten Sushi Ginza Onodera,

00:08:52.958 --> 00:08:56.250
a more casual branch of the Michelin-starred
Onodera group.

00:08:58.625 --> 00:09:01.541
You order from a tablet like most
other conveyor belt places,

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:04.000
but the quality here really stands out.

00:09:04.916 --> 00:09:09.375
It's high-end, Edomae-style sushi at
a more approachable price.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.166
My personal favorites was the Chutoro

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.416
and the Otoro Nigiri.

00:09:37.083 --> 00:09:40.750
This is by far the best conveyor
belt sushi spot in Tokyo.

00:09:44.458 --> 00:09:47.916
Next, we head to Shinjuku
and make our way to

00:09:47.916 --> 00:09:51.708
Kabukicho, Shinjuku's neon-splashed sleepless town.

00:09:57.125 --> 00:09:59.958
Cramming in well over 3,000
bars, clubs, and

00:09:59.958 --> 00:10:03.500
love hotels, it's Japan's largest
entertainment district.

00:10:07.166 --> 00:10:09.083
Stick to the lit main drags, ignore

00:10:09.083 --> 00:10:12.125
aggressive promoters, and soak
up the electric signage

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:14.916
that inspired the movie Blade Runner, or

00:10:14.916 --> 00:10:18.708
try to find the Godzilla head
located above the Gracery Hotel.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.000
Just around the corner, look
up at the 3D giant cat.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:34.250
It usually shows up every few minutes
between 7am and 1am.

00:10:35.708 --> 00:10:37.666
Next, head to Omoide Yokocho.

00:10:38.541 --> 00:10:41.375
A small narrow alleyway,
often referred to

00:10:41.375 --> 00:10:43.791
as "piss alley" due to the
lack of toilets

00:10:43.958 --> 00:10:44.583
back in the day.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:50.666
It's now packed with tiny smoky izakayas
and busy 8-seat noodle joints.

00:11:01.583 --> 00:11:06.500
Expect narrow passages, hanging lanterns,
and mostly a cash-only service.

00:11:07.416 --> 00:11:09.833
Drop by after 5pm for peak hours.

00:11:13.958 --> 00:11:16.500
Towering above it all is Tokyo Kabukicho tower,

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.500
a 48-story entertainment
complex that opened

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:20.541
in 2023.

00:11:21.125 --> 00:11:22.750
On the ground floor, you'll
find lively food

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:25.166
hall with a mix of casual
eateries, and just

00:11:25.166 --> 00:11:27.458
above is the arcade floor,
which is packed

00:11:27.458 --> 00:11:30.333
with video games, crane games, and rhythm

00:11:30.416 --> 00:11:30.916
machines.

00:11:35.375 --> 00:11:37.583
Later in the evening, hop
over to Golden Gai.

00:11:38.166 --> 00:11:40.125
Here you'll find six alleyways crammed

00:11:40.125 --> 00:11:42.166
with roughly 200 microbars,

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.208
many seating fewer than 10 patrons.

00:11:45.625 --> 00:11:47.916
Each spot has its own theme, punk, jazz,

00:11:48.125 --> 00:11:51.166
and movie posters, and a small
cover charge is normal.

00:11:51.833 --> 00:11:54.958
Things don't really get going until
9pm, so do pace yourself.

00:12:09.291 --> 00:12:12.208
For a breather, slip into Hanazono Shrine,

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:20.375
The shrine is open 24 hours,
but go by day

00:12:20.375 --> 00:12:22.500
to admire the bright vermilion
torii gates,

00:12:23.041 --> 00:12:24.833
especially during the cherry
blossom season.

00:12:30.541 --> 00:12:32.166
Next, head to Shinjuku Gyoen.

00:12:33.041 --> 00:12:35.833
In late March and early
April, about 1,000

00:12:35.833 --> 00:12:38.333
cherry trees burst into bloom, giving you

00:12:38.333 --> 00:12:40.625
a longer sakura window than most parks.

00:12:52.166 --> 00:12:56.375
It's extremely popular amongst locals and tourists,
so make sure to book tickets in

00:12:56.375 --> 00:12:59.125
advance if you want to visit during
the cherry blossom season.

00:13:01.708 --> 00:13:05.958
Finally, refuel at Fuunji, a
legendary tsukemen shop.

00:13:10.166 --> 00:13:13.708
A queue of 30-45 minutes is typical
for this place.

00:13:20.083 --> 00:13:22.333
As a reward for your waiting,
you're offered

00:13:22.333 --> 00:13:25.291
a plate of noodles and a
deeply-rich chicken

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.041
and bonito dipping broth.

00:13:38.375 --> 00:13:41.416
Every slurp the flavors intensify, until

00:13:41.416 --> 00:13:43.916
you're wondering why you're
ever going to settle

00:13:44.125 --> 00:13:46.708
for regular ramen ever again.

00:13:53.875 --> 00:13:56.000
Next, we head to the Taito Ward.

00:13:57.666 --> 00:14:03.250
More specifically, the area of Asakusa where
we begin with the Senso-ji Temple.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:10.250
Senso-ji is Tokyo's oldest and
most iconic temple.

00:14:12.208 --> 00:14:14.125
Your visit starts at Kaminarimon,

00:14:14.333 --> 00:14:17.125
the Grand Thunder Gate, 
marked by its massive red lantern.

00:14:17.916 --> 00:14:20.041
It's one of the most photographed
spots in the city.

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:25.791
This past the gate is Nakamise Street, a

00:14:25.791 --> 00:14:28.000
lively shopping lane packed with
traditional snacks,

00:14:28.416 --> 00:14:30.416
souvenirs, and speciality shops.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:34.625
One unique stop here is Yoroshi
Cosmetics, a

00:14:34.625 --> 00:14:37.083
90-year-old beauty store where
you can literally

00:14:37.125 --> 00:14:39.791
buy a hand cream for every
day of the year.

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:49.583
As you walk deeper into the temple grounds,

00:14:49.791 --> 00:14:51.750
you'll pass through
a cloud of purifying

00:14:51.791 --> 00:14:54.375
incense smoke, rising from
a giant cauldron.

00:14:55.041 --> 00:14:59.416
You'll find locals wave it toward their heads
and bodies for good luck and health.

00:15:05.125 --> 00:15:09.208
Don't miss trying your luck with an omikuji,
a traditional paper fortune.

00:15:11.666 --> 00:15:13.750
Just shake the metal box,
pull out a stick,

00:15:14.458 --> 00:15:16.666
and match the number to your
fortune drawer.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.833
Remember to tie up any bad fortunes
before leaving.

00:15:30.291 --> 00:15:35.208
For a completely different experience, consider
coming back to Senso-ji at night.

00:15:36.875 --> 00:15:40.583
During the evening, the crowds disappear
and the lanterns glow softly.

00:15:44.875 --> 00:15:48.541
After Senso-ji, explore the nearby side
streets for a bite to eat.

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:52.208
There are tiny eateries around serving

00:15:52.208 --> 00:15:55.416
grilled unagi onigiri, a smoky
eel rice ball.

00:15:59.458 --> 00:16:04.625
Or you can try a curry bread, deep-fried with
a golden crunch and savory filling.

00:16:18.083 --> 00:16:22.791
And finally, make sure to visit Kappabashi
Street, Tokyo's legendary kitchen town.

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:25.708
Whether you're looking for
pro-grade knives,

00:16:26.125 --> 00:16:28.666
handmade ceramics, or those
ultra-realistic

00:16:28.708 --> 00:16:32.750
plastic food models, it's a treasure
trove for anyone who loves cooking.

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:38.208
We managed to pick up a couple of bowls
and plates to bring home with us.

00:16:52.208 --> 00:16:56.583
If you're in the area, Tokyo's Skytree is
worth a visit, especially for the view.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:00.083
Standing at 634 meters, it's the tallest

00:17:00.083 --> 00:17:02.750
structure in Japan and offers
sweeping panoramas

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:04.791
of the city from its two
observation decks.

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:08.083
Next door from the Skytree, we visit

00:17:08.083 --> 00:17:13.583
Kaiten Sushi Toriton, another top-rated
conveyor belt sushi spot

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:43.208
Next, we head to the northeastern part of Chiyoda Ward, 

00:17:44.666 --> 00:17:46.541
specifically
for Akihabara.

00:17:48.125 --> 00:17:53.416
Akihabara is Tokyo's mecha for all things
anime, gaming, and otaku culture.

00:17:54.333 --> 00:17:56.333
It's a hypersaturated playground for

00:17:56.333 --> 00:17:57.083
character goods,

00:17:58.083 --> 00:17:59.291
multi-level arcades,

00:17:59.916 --> 00:18:01.166
and maid cafes.

00:18:02.791 --> 00:18:07.041
If you're into pop culture even a little,
it's easy to spend an entire day here.

00:18:30.541 --> 00:18:34.791
Start at the iconic Radio Kaikan, one of
the district's landmark buildings.

00:18:35.666 --> 00:18:37.500
This 10-floor building is packed with

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:42.000
collectible figures, model kits,
training cards, and rare merch.

00:18:42.791 --> 00:18:46.375
It's chaotic, colorful, and dense
with fandom energy.

00:18:47.416 --> 00:18:51.458
You can easily spend hours in here,
exploring every nook and cranny.

00:19:07.166 --> 00:19:11.791
A few steps away is Kotobukiya, another
must-visit spot for figure lovers.

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:15.416
The store sells high-quality, licensed

00:19:15.416 --> 00:19:18.708
goods from franchises like
Final Fantasy, Zelda,

00:19:19.250 --> 00:19:20.125
and Studio Ghibli.

00:19:21.208 --> 00:19:25.833
They also often carry unique Japan-exclusive
items you won't find anywhere else.

00:19:34.458 --> 00:19:37.291
Next, dive into the GiGO arcades, which

00:19:37.291 --> 00:19:39.916
dominates the main street with
floor after floor of

00:19:39.958 --> 00:19:43.125
crane games, rhythm machines,
and fighting games.

00:19:45.208 --> 00:19:47.125
Even if you're not planning to play, it's

00:19:47.125 --> 00:19:49.250
worth stepping inside just
to experience the

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:53.666
noise, the lights, and pure sensory
overload that defines Akihabara.

00:20:03.708 --> 00:20:06.666
For a theme break, visit one of the
neighborhood's concept cafes.

00:20:10.166 --> 00:20:12.541
For me personally, there are
two standout options.

00:20:13.083 --> 00:20:17.916
The Monster Hunter bar, styled after Capcom's
monster-slaying fantasy world.

00:20:19.041 --> 00:20:23.166
Here you can find menu items inspired by
the in-game foods and monsters.

00:20:25.041 --> 00:20:26.875
The space is decorated with
Monster Hunter

00:20:26.875 --> 00:20:29.291
relics, character armors,
and you can even

00:20:29.291 --> 00:20:30.750
play the game if you want to.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:35.791
There's also the Eorzea
Cafe, themed after

00:20:35.791 --> 00:20:38.750
Final Fantasy XIV, with detailed interior

00:20:38.791 --> 00:20:42.916
design and dishes based on the massive
multiplayer online game.

00:20:43.291 --> 00:20:46.000
Reservations are recommended, especially
on the weekends.

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:51.458
If you're a retro gaming fan, then
Super Potato is unmissable.

00:20:52.083 --> 00:20:54.750
This nostalgic shop is stacked
wall-to-wall

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:58.166
with Famicom catridges, Game
Boys, and early

00:20:58.166 --> 00:20:59.000
PlayStation titles.

00:21:00.041 --> 00:21:02.375
It almost feels more like
a museum for the

00:21:02.375 --> 00:21:05.291
golden age of gaming than
a retro arcade shop.

00:21:11.416 --> 00:21:13.458
On the fifth floor, you'll find a tiny

00:21:13.458 --> 00:21:16.375
arcade where you can play pixel-era
classics like

00:21:16.375 --> 00:21:18.208
Metal Slug or Street Fighter.

00:21:23.916 --> 00:21:26.750
And you can even refuel yourself
with some Japanese candy.

00:21:38.625 --> 00:21:41.708
In the morning, head to the southern
edge of the Chiyoda Ward.

00:21:43.416 --> 00:21:45.458
Here you'll find the Hie Shrine.

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:55.625
The shrine is dedicated to the Oyamakui no
Kami, the mountain deity of Mount Hiei.

00:21:58.875 --> 00:22:01.583
It features a captivating
back entrance, a

00:22:01.583 --> 00:22:03.958
tunnel of around 90 vermillion torii gates

00:22:04.333 --> 00:22:07.500
that mirrors Kyoto's iconic
Fushimi Inari Shrine.

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:27.458
We then head to the Chuo Ward

00:22:29.458 --> 00:22:32.208
and visit the Tsukiji Outer Market.

00:22:40.791 --> 00:22:43.916
The market is a maze of street food
stalls and restaurants.

00:22:44.625 --> 00:22:49.041
You weave through narrow lanes lined with
sizzling grills and fresh sashimi.

00:22:49.458 --> 00:22:51.375
It's the kind of place where
you simply follow

00:22:51.375 --> 00:22:54.166
your nose and stop when something
smells good.

00:22:54.833 --> 00:22:57.541
We start at Yamocho, where
the tamagoyaki

00:22:57.541 --> 00:23:00.083
comes hot off the griddle, sweet, fluffy,

00:23:00.458 --> 00:23:02.333
and perfect for a breakfast on the go.

00:23:08.541 --> 00:23:11.291
If you stall down, grab a
strawberry daifuku,

00:23:11.833 --> 00:23:14.875
a whole berry wrapped in
red bean paste, and

00:23:14.875 --> 00:23:16.125
cloud soft mochi.

00:23:22.458 --> 00:23:24.250
Keep wandering until you spot the press

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:28.500
that turns an entire octopus
into a crispy Tako Senbei,

00:23:28.833 --> 00:23:34.125
the size of a dinner plate, salty,
smoky, and unexpectedly addictive.

00:23:54.041 --> 00:23:57.708
However, the main reason we're here
is to grab lunch at Seagen.

00:24:01.666 --> 00:24:03.625
Order the ultimate tuna bowl

00:24:03.916 --> 00:24:07.208
and watch the chef layer lean, 
medium fatty, and minced

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:09.625
tuna in a bowl with rice underneath.

00:24:10.541 --> 00:24:13.166
It's a meal that could easily feed two

00:24:13.166 --> 00:24:16.250
it's generous, filling, and totally
worth the wait.

00:24:34.166 --> 00:24:35.833
Next, hop over to Ginza.

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:38.375
If you're in the mood to splurge, then

00:24:38.375 --> 00:24:40.541
browse the flagship stores along
the main street.

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:45.708
Or head over to the Art Aquarium Museum.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:53.250
You'll find hundreds of goldfish
drift through

00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:55.958
kaleidoscopic tanks that feels
more like modern

00:24:56.166 --> 00:24:58.416
art than actual aquariums.

00:25:13.375 --> 00:25:16.041
For the best view of Tokyo
Tower, ride the

00:25:16.041 --> 00:25:18.916
lift to the Sky Lobby on
the 33rd floor of

00:25:18.916 --> 00:25:20.000
Azabudai Hills.

00:25:20.375 --> 00:25:22.250
It's no longer free entry, and you're

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:24.333
required to order at least one
drink from one of the

00:25:24.375 --> 00:25:27.000
cafes at the Sky Lobby, but
there's no other

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:29.500
observatory that offers a
better view of the

00:25:29.500 --> 00:25:30.208
iconic tower.

00:25:31.541 --> 00:25:35.291
While you're in Azabudai Hills, don't
miss out on Team Lab Borderless.

00:25:36.541 --> 00:25:38.625
The new location spreads its interactive

00:25:38.625 --> 00:25:41.375
light installations across multiple
floors,

00:25:41.791 --> 00:25:44.250
rooms blend into one another 
so you can wander

00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:46.958
for an hour and feel like
you stepped inside a

00:25:46.958 --> 00:25:47.666
living painting.

00:26:04.916 --> 00:26:08.666
If you intend to visit, then booking ahead
is smart, especially on the weekends.

00:26:10.833 --> 00:26:13.750
From there, make your way to
Tokyo Tower itself.

00:26:18.416 --> 00:26:20.291
Step inside Red Tokyo Tower.

00:26:20.833 --> 00:26:25.583
Inside you'll find VR Rides, Sim Racing
Rigs, and Esports Arenas.

00:26:26.041 --> 00:26:30.208
Basically an adult playground wrapped
in the tower's iconic red steel.

00:26:53.458 --> 00:26:58.250
Cap the evening off with a world-class
yakiniku experience at Yoroniku.

00:27:05.333 --> 00:27:07.541
You'll be seated in a private
room, where a

00:27:07.541 --> 00:27:10.583
staff member expertly grills
each cut tableside.

00:27:46.958 --> 00:27:48.708
The highlight of the meal was the

00:27:48.708 --> 00:27:52.750
Katsusando, an ultra-tender wagyu
cutlet with a light

00:27:52.791 --> 00:27:54.541
barely-there panko crust.

00:27:55.375 --> 00:27:57.750
Nestled between soft crustless
white bread,

00:27:58.083 --> 00:28:01.166
the beef was so buttery and
rich, it practically

00:28:01.375 --> 00:28:03.333
melted the moment you took a bite.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:16.291
Alright that's a wrap on our whirlwind
tour of Tokyo.

00:28:16.791 --> 00:28:18.791
If you made it this far, thank you.

00:28:18.791 --> 00:28:21.416
I know these videos can be super dense so

00:28:21.416 --> 00:28:23.750
the fact that you stuck around
means a lot.

00:28:24.208 --> 00:28:26.666
From dodging the crowds at
Shibuya Crossing

00:28:26.666 --> 00:28:29.958
to taking that peaceful late-night
walk through Senso-ji,

00:28:30.083 --> 00:28:34.541
every stop gave me one of those
"wow, I'm really here" kind of moments.

00:28:35.125 --> 00:28:38.125
I hope the way I shared it helps
you find a few of your own.

00:28:39.208 --> 00:28:43.083
If any of these spots end up on your trip,
let me know in the comments below.

00:28:43.541 --> 00:28:46.333
And if you got any hidden gems that
I missed, drop those too.

00:28:46.666 --> 00:28:48.625
I'm always planning the next Tokyo visit.

00:28:49.333 --> 00:28:50.583
Thanks again for watching this video.

00:28:51.041 --> 00:28:54.333
This is Allan, safe travels, and I'll
see you guys in the next one.

